# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so a clone
# there checks every file out with CRLF. Everything in this repo is read by
# something that wants LF: bash chokes on the \r in a CRLF .bashrc, and on
# Windows ~/.bashrc is a symlink straight into this working tree, so the
# checked-out form is the form that runs. The index has always been LF; this
# just stops the worktree diverging from it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exception. These are read by PowerToys rather than by bash, and they are
# copied into place by powertoys-config.sh rather than symlinked, so nothing
# here needs LF. PowerToys writes editorSettings.json with CRLF and none of
# them with a trailing newline; normalising would mean a whole-file diff every
# time PowerToys saved, which is the opposite of why they are tracked. -text
# leaves them exactly as written.
windows/powertoys/** -text
